## Free prescriptions

In **England**, if you need thyroid hormone replacement, such as levothyroxine, you can get a **medical exemption certificate**. This gives you **free prescriptions for all your medicines**, not just your thyroid tablets. Ask your GP surgery for the form (called FP92A). Prescriptions are **free for everyone in Wales**.

## Your rights at work

An underactive thyroid can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life, judged as if you were not taking treatment. If so, your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- time off for **appointments and blood tests**
- **flexible hours** while your dose is being adjusted
- a **phased return** after time off

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your health affects your daily life. Most people with a treated underactive thyroid do not qualify on this condition alone. But if you have ongoing symptoms, or other conditions as well, they may affect activities such as preparing food, washing or moving around.

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

If you cannot work because of your health, you may be able to get **Universal Credit** or **New Style Employment and Support Allowance**. A free adviser, such as Citizens Advice, can help you check.
